DEFAULT_EXCLUDES carried the bare names venv / .venv / env, and the matcher
applies an exact name to EVERY component of the relative path at any depth.
So every directory named `env` in every repository was deleted before
analysis. Measured across 467 corpus repos:

  427 source files, 77 directories, 39 repos (8.4%)
  425 of 427 (99.5%) FIRST_PARTY per supply_chain.classify_file
  0 of the 110 venv-NAMED dirs in the corpus carry any virtualenv marker

The worst two are whole subsystems and both sit at the repo root: RocksDB's
POSIX I/O layer (env_posix.cc, fs_posix.cc, io_posix.cc, file_system.cc) and
U-Boot's environment storage subsystem (env.c, ext4.c, fat.c, mmc.c, nand.c).
The deleted code is systematically I/O-dense, which is the worst thing for an
exclusion list to hide from a boundary analysis: on poetry the dropped package
is its virtualenv MANAGER, and re-including it takes unique I/O boundary chains
70 -> 107 (+37, +52.9%) and total_io_edges 59 -> 96, with zero chains lost.
Independently reproduced: 107 chains, exactly 37 anchored in an env/ path
(env_read 24, fs_read 7, subprocess 5, fs_write 1).

Exclusion is now conditioned on PEP 405's pyvenv.cfg, or bin/activate /
Scripts/activate for older tooling.

ROOT-ANCHORING WAS EVALUATED AND REJECTED ON MEASUREMENT. `env/*` already works
in this matcher and needs no new machinery, but it recovers 379 of 427 (88.8%)
and structurally cannot recover rocksdb's and U-Boot's env/, which are at depth
0 -- precisely where a virtualenv would be. Only content separates them.
Conversely `vendor` must stay unanchored: 19,251 of its 124,721 files sit below
the root and are genuine dependencies, so that exclusion is doing correct work.

ONE RULE, EVERY WALKER -- AND THE DRIFT HAPPENED DURING THE CHANGE. This module
has two independent tree walkers: FileIndex.build's os.walk prune loop (the fast
path every real run takes) and _is_excluded_classified. The venv rule was added
to one, and find_files then excluded a virtualenv correctly while a full
run_behavior_map still analysed it -- the same input, two answers. Caught by the
suite, not by review. Both now call is_pruned_dir, and
test_all_three_walkers_agree asserts parity BEHAVIOURALLY across all three
public entry points rather than by grepping for the call, so a fourth copy that
merely looks right cannot satisfy it.

Three existing tests were invalidated and their FIXTURES were fixed, not their
assertions: test_excludes_venv / test_excludes_dot_venv built a venv/ holding
only a lib/ tree, which is no longer a virtualenv, so analysing it is correct.
Each grew a real marker (one pyvenv.cfg, one bin/activate, so both arms run
end-to-end) and keeps asserting its original property.

SCOPE HELD. No discriminator was evaluated for build (1,965 files / 65 repos),
coverage (246), target (1,183), out, dist, site, .cache -- two of them larger
than env -- and they are NOT fixed here on the strength of the env result.
The Scripts/activate arm has NO real-world positive control (zero corpus hits,
no Windows virtualenv available); it ships pinned by fixture and is not claimed
validated. The corpus behind "0 of 110" holds no installed virtualenvs at all
(467 bare clones), so that zero is partly a corpus property -- the live control
is this repository's own .venv, asserted still-excluded against the real dir.

